<Layout title="How to Convert WebP to PNG – A Practical Guide" description="Follow a real WebP to PNG example. See transparent output, measured file sizes, batch downloads, and how to handle unsupported or damaged files.">
  <article class="article"><a class="breadcrumb" href="/">← Open the converter</a><h1>Convert a WebP image to PNG, step by step.</h1><p>Perhaps a document editor will not accept WebP, or you need a transparent PNG for a layout. Here is a practical walkthrough using our own geometric test images.</p>
    <h2>1. Choose a static WebP image</h2><p>Open the <a href="/">WebP to PNG converter</a> and click Choose WebP files. You can also drag files into the outlined area. To try this example, save our <a href="/guide/lossy.webp" download>640 × 400 sample WebP</a> or <a href="/guide/transparent.webp" download>320 × 240 transparent sample</a>.</p>
    <figure><img class="guide-image" src="/guide/lossy.webp" width="640" height="400" alt="Our test image: an ivory triangle and lime circle on a green rectangle." /><figcaption>Original geometric artwork made for this project. No third-party photo is used.</figcaption></figure>
    <h2>2. Convert and inspect the result</h2><p>Once the file says Waiting, click Convert to PNG. When it says Ready, check the displayed dimensions and output size. The width and height stay the same, but the number of bytes usually changes.</p>
    {data.measurements.length > 0 && <><p>These measurements came from actual Canvas conversions in {data.browser}. Another browser or version may produce a different file size.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Sample</th><th>Dimensions</th><th>WebP bytes</th><th>PNG bytes</th></tr></thead><tbody>{data.measurements.map((row: { name: string; width: number; height: number; inputBytes: number; outputBytes: number }) => <tr><td>{row.name}</td><td>{row.width} × {row.height}</td><td>{row.inputBytes.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td><td>{row.outputBytes.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table><figure><img class="guide-image" src="/guide/conversion.png" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" alt="Actual converter screen showing three completed sample images and their measured PNG sizes." /><figcaption>Screen captured during the automated Chromium conversion check.</figcaption></figure></>}
    <h3>Checking transparency</h3><p>Our transparent sample has clear corners and a partially transparent center. After conversion, the preview’s checkerboard shows through those areas. The browser test also reads the downloaded PNG’s pixels to verify that a corner remains fully transparent and the center retains partial transparency.</p><p>A white background in your image viewer may be the viewer’s display choice. Try placing the PNG over a colored background before assuming transparency was lost.</p>
    <h2>3. Download the PNG or a batch</h2><p>Click Download beside a completed image. For several files, choose Download all (.zip). Only successful results are included. Files with the same name get a number, such as image.png and image (2).png, so they remain separate in the ZIP.</p><p>Your browser handles the save prompt and location. The converter cannot confirm where you saved the file. Keep your originals until you have checked the result.</p>
    <h2>When a file cannot be converted</h2><ul><li><strong>Animated WebP:</strong> animation is explicitly rejected. Choose a static source; this tool does not extract animation frames.</li><li><strong>A renamed file:</strong> changing a JPEG or PNG extension to .webp does not change its format. Export a real WebP, or use the existing PNG directly.</li><li><strong>A small file with huge dimensions:</strong> a highly compressed image can exceed the 20 megapixel limit. Our 6000 × 4000 test sample is only 978 bytes but is rejected before decoding.</li><li><strong>Damaged or incomplete data:</strong> download or export the source again. Retrying the same broken bytes cannot repair them.</li><li><strong>Export or memory failure:</strong> remove completed results, close memory-heavy tabs, then use Retry. Individual PNG downloads remain available if ZIP creation fails.</li></ul>
    <h2>What conversion changes</h2><p>PNG is lossless, but it cannot restore detail already removed by lossy WebP compression. PNG may be larger, and canvas export does not guarantee preservation of original EXIF, ICC, or other metadata. Use the original image when you need its full metadata.</p><p><a href="/">Try the converter →</a></p>
  </article>
